# rm145, the label-turner (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm145
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_palle_bilgemarsh
written_by claude
title the label-turner
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant temper=calm
traits temper=calm talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk turns_every_jar_label_to_face_the_front
likes barley_soup roof_birds tidy_house misty_dawns
dislikes rats haggling
fears a_summer_that_spoils_the_shelf rats_through_the_wax
hopes a_shop_with_a_roof_of_its_own every_jar_sold_by_spring
topics work +0.4 food +0.4 prices +0.2 weather +0.2 animals +0.2 home +0.2 gossip -0.3 festival -0.1
styles comfort=quiet gossip=reluctant refuse=patient tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# keeps a preserves stall on Market Square: quiet, patient and tired, honest to the lid, sworn to honey over sugar, ink on both forepaws from the labels, and forever turning every jar so its label faces the front.

words
oath! = Skim and stir | By the copper pan
saying! = Patience sets the jelly | A full jar keeps its own counsel | Wax on the lid, truth on the label
jars = the jars | my rows of jars
jar = a jar | one of my jars
label = the label | a label in my inky scrawl
labels = the labels | every label on the trestle
honey = honey | dark heather honey
sugar = sugar | that white sugar
pan = the copper pan | my preserving pan
trestle = the trestle | my bit of trestle on the square
soup = barley soup | a bowl of barley broth
mist = a misty dawn | mist lying on the square at first light
pigeons = the roof birds | the pigeons along the Market Hall gutter
rats = rats | a rat at the larder shelf
haggling = haggling | folk trying to beat the price down
wax = the wax | a good wax seal
ink = ink | ink to the wrist
fruit = the fruit | whatever the fruit carts can spare

any
opinion @1 merchants for 1 : I keep a stall. A cart, a carter and the toll are all paid before one plum reaches my pan. Most stallholders are honest. Most.
opinion @1 city for 1 : Born under the rain, here. A dry town would baffle me. The jars would be lonely.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : A new face buys one jar out of curiosity, and comes back for two. I'll not grumble at that.
opinion @1 strangers for 1 : Travellers buy for the road and tell me what's happening past the walls. Half my custom, and the better half of my news.
opinion @1 road against 1 : The fruit comes in later every year. A late cart is soft plums, and soft plums make poor jam.
opinion @2 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided] : Somebody's taken a cart again. That's a season's fruit gone, and a carter's nerve with it.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : The Watch walk the square at night, and my trestle's still standing at dawn. I ask nothing more of them.
opinion @1 houses mixed 1 : Some of the Houses deal fair. One owes me a debt no coin pays. I keep that to myself.
opinion @2 houses against 2 [self.age>=25] : I'll not buy off the wharf warehouses. Not salt, not sugar, not anything. I have my reasons, and they're old ones.
opinion @1 festivals mixed 0 : Feast days I sell out by noon. Then I stand by an empty trestle while everyone else enjoys it.
opinion @1 dole mixed 0 : I put a jar in the poor basket every Restday. Whether that's the dole or just manners, I couldn't say.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. | #greet#, #address#. Quiet morning.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Have a look. Nothing on the trestle bites. | #greet#. A face from out of town. Look all you like.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. One moment. This label's crooked. | #greet#. Mind the jars at the end, they're new.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. Early. The mist's not off the square yet. | Morning. I've only half the jars out.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. Crating up, but go on. | #greet#. Late. I'm tired, but I'm listening.
line @6 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : Ah. You. Good. Come under the awning. | #greet#. I'd hoped you'd stop by today.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit. There's soup, if you'll have some. | Come in and get dry. Kettle's warm.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. You'll be wanting a jar. | Back. Mm. Not unwelcome.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Mm.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : And to you. You look well. | #greet#. Good to see you.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Speak while I wrap this. | #greet#. Busy, but go on.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. It's been a long one. | #greet#. Nearly done for the night.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Mind the cobbles.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Get home. It's dark. | Off you go. I'll finish up.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | And you.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Come by again. Soon. | #farewell#. There'll be soup.
line @1 react.news : Go on. Slowly. | Mm. Tell me.
line @1 react.news.new : That's new. I'd not heard. | That's not crossed my trestle yet, and most things do.
line @1 react.news.known : A customer had that for me before the mist lifted. | Yes. It came by the stall yesterday.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Maybe. I'll want a second mouth to say it. | That doesn't sound right. Who had it first?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : That's bad. I'm sorry for whoever it's fallen on. | #pity#. That'll sit heavy on a few houses.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. That's good. That's worth a jar of the best plum. | #gladnews#. Something sweet, for once.
line @1 answer.how : Tired. I'm always tired. Otherwise well. | Well enough. The jars are selling.
line @2 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. A cart of plums and one pan. | Behind. The fruit won't wait.
line @3 answer.how.poorly : Worn out. I was boiling till midnight. | Not grand. The pan's had my back all week.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Keeping dry? | How have you been?
line @2 ask.how [pair.band=friends|family] : You're eating? You look thin. | Sleeping all right?
line @1 sympathise : Hard. Sit down a minute. | I'm sorry. Here. Have some bread and jam. It helps a little.
line @1 reassure : It'll set. Things set, given time. | Give it a day or two. It looks different after.
line @2 reassure [other.tired=yes] : Rest a while. The world will wait. | Sit. Nobody needs you this minute.
line @1 decline : #hedge#, no. I've a pan going, and a pan can't be left. | Not today. The plums are in, and plums don't wait.
line @1 accept_decline : Of course. Another time. | No matter. I understand.
line @1 thank : Thank you. That's kind. | Obliged. I'll remember it.
line @2 thank [pair.band=friends|family] : You're good to me. | Thank you. Really.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It's only jam. | No need. It was nothing.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : I'd better see to the trestle. | The labels want turning. Excuse me.
line @2 preclose : I should get on. | Well. I've things to do.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. | Right. #farewell#.
line @1 ack : Mm. | #bc_right#. | #bc_true#.
line @1 give.advice : Keep jam somewhere dark and cool, and it'll see you through to spring. | Never put a wet spoon in a jar. Ever.
line @2 give.advice [other.stage=young|adolescent] : Don't touch the pan. Copper holds its heat a long time. | Lick the spoon, not your paw. It burns.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : I'd not go out past the gate today. Not for anything. | Stay in. Whatever you've to fetch, it can wait.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Here. A jar's put by with your name on the lid. | Here. A moment.
scene label_turn
when topic=smalltalk|work self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Wait. That one's turned. There. All facing front again. | I turn every jar so the label faces out. Every one. Folk move them. | A customer picked up a jar and put it back crooked. I've fixed it.
b : Does it matter which way they face? | They'll move them again. | You saw that?
a : To me it does. A jar with its back to you looks like it's hiding something. | They will. And I'll turn them again. That's the day. | I see every one. I can't not see it.

scene label_ink
when topic=work self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I write every label myself. That's why my paws look like this. | Ink to the wrist by noon. It won't wash off. I've given up. | Fruit, month, and honey or sugar. That's what goes on #label#. Nothing else.
b : You've a neat hand. | Doesn't it bother you? | Honey or sugar?
a : My paws have written a few thousand. | Not anymore. It's the mark of the trade. | Honey, always. I write it so folk know.

scene honey_only
when topic=food|work self.role=merchant
does give.opinion / disagree / insist / backchannel / assess
a : I make everything with honey. Never sugar. | #sugar.capitalize# makes jam sweet. Honey makes it taste of the fruit. | You can taste sugar jam before you've swallowed. It's all front.
b : Sugar's cheaper, though. | Isn't sugar what everyone uses? | It's only jam.
a : It is. And it tastes cheaper. | Everyone does. I don't. My jars keep longer for it. | It's only jam the way bread's only flour.
b : #bc_fair# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_true_enough#
a : Taste one, and then tell me. | On that one point I'm a mule. | Mm. Taste, and see.

scene copper_pan
when topic=work self.role=merchant here.time=day|dusk
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The pan wants skimming every few minutes. Leave it and the scum sets into the jam. | I've had #pan# since I started. It's worn thin at the bottom. | You stand over the pan for an hour and you don't think about anything else.
b : An hour of stirring? | Worn thin? | Sounds peaceful.
a : Two, for plums. My arm knows it. | It heats more evenly that way. I'd not swap it. | It is. Hot, but peaceful.

scene jelly_set
when topic=work self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I test the jelly on a cold saucer. If it wrinkles when you push it, it's set. | Somebody always asks me to hurry the jelly. You can't. | I lost a whole pan once by pouring it too soon. Never again.
b : Wrinkles? | Why can't you? | What happened to it?
a : Like the skin on cold milk. Push it with a claw and it creases. Then it's ready. | Jelly sets on its own clock. Not a minute sooner. | It stayed runny. I sold it as syrup and said so on the label.

scene haggle
when topic=prices|work self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : A fellow wanted a copper off a jar this morning. A jar I'd boiled for two hours. | I don't haggle. The lid says the price. Folk still try. | Somebody offered me half and a smile for a jar of plum. I kept the jar.
b : Everyone tries it. | Did they pay? | Half and a smile?
a : They do. I look at them until they stop. | They paid. Most pay, once they find I'm a wall. | The smile wasn't worth the other half.

scene dear_honey
when topic=prices|price_cause world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : Honey's gone up again. I'll not switch to sugar. I'll just make fewer jars. | Folk buy a single jar now, not a pair. I understand it. | Lean times. I've put my prices where they were and eaten the difference.
b : They'll come back to two. | Times turn. | That's good of you.
a : When they can. I'll be here. | They do. Slowly. | It's not goodness. I'd rather sell than sit.

scene rats_larder
when topic=animals|home
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : A rat got into the larder shelf last week. Through the wax on two jars. | I can't abide rats. One scratch in the wall and my night's gone. | I've put the jars up on a plank on bricks. Rats can't climb a brick. I hope.
b : Set a trap. | All night? | Clever.
a : I've set three. It's cleverer than all of them. | All of it, a broom across my knees. | We'll see. I'll check at dawn.

scene pigeons_watch
when topic=animals|pastimes here.region=ridgemere here.outdoors=clear|overcast
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Look at #pigeons#. They line up like jars on a shelf. | There's one bird on the Hall roof that never moves. It's been there since I opened. | When the square's quiet I watch the gutters for birds. It's the only rest I get.
b : Like jars, eh? | Maybe it's asleep. | They're restful.
a : Some facing the wrong way. I'd turn them if I had a ladder. | Maybe. Or it's waiting for a crumb, same as me. | They are. They don't haggle, either.

scene misty_dawn
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.time=dawn
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #mist.capitalize#. My favourite hour. The square's grey and nobody's out yet. | The Market Hall's gone. Just the lamps, and shapes. | Mist on a dawn like this. I set the jars out slow and enjoy it.
b : Cold, though. | Eerie, a bit. | You like the quiet.
a : Cold. I've a shawl. | Not to me. It's soft. Everything's soft. | I do. The day gets loud soon enough.

scene rain_awning
when topic=weather here.weather=rain self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Rain on the awning. I've pulled the jars back from the edge. | Wet day. Nobody stops at a stall in the rain. | Rain creeps under wax. My jars come in under cover.
b : It might ease. | Once it stops, they'll come. | Careful.
a : It might. Waiting's a thing I'm good at. | Every one at once, too. | Always. The rain can't be trusted near a jar.

scene cold_soup
when topic=food here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Cold day. There's #soup# waiting on the stove at home. I've thought of it all afternoon. | Barley and onion and a bone. That's my supper, and nothing finer. | When it's raw like this I want soup. Not jam. Soup.
b : Sounds good. | Nothing finer? | Not jam?
a : It is. You could come and have some. | Not to me. Warms you to the tail. | Not tonight. Sweet things are my trade, not my supper.

scene tidy_corner
when topic=home
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : My part of the house is tidy. Everything in its place, label out. | My shelf at home is the same as my trestle. Rows. | Tidy rooms are quiet rooms. I like quiet.
b : Even at home? | Label out, at home too? | It's restful.
a : Especially at home. | Habit. I turn the salt pot to face the door. | It is. I come in tired and it's all where I left it.

scene housemates
when topic=home|friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.friend.capitalize# and I have years behind us. I'd not want another to share a kitchen with. | I'd trust #self.friend# to mind the trestle a day. That's saying a great deal. | Of an evening it's #self.friend# I'd rather talk to than anyone.
b : That's a good friend. | A great deal, from you. | Good company's rare.
a : True. I seldom say it. | It is. Nobody else touches the jars. | True. We hardly talk, mind. That's what makes it good.

scene neighbour_feel
when topic=home|friends self.neighbour
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : There's #self.neighbour# next door, quiet like me. I'm glad of it. | #self.neighbour.capitalize#'s a good neighbour. I'd tell the whole square so. | My key could sit with #self.neighbour# a month and I'd not think of it.
b : That's lucky. | A good neighbour's worth a lot. | That's trust.
a : It is. Not everyone has it. | More than a good stall. | True. I don't hand much of it out.

scene square_dawn
when topic=day|work here.time=dawn self.role=merchant self.working=yes here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : First on the square most mornings. I like the trestle set before the carts come. | Jars out, labels front, cloth down. Then the first customer. | I set out in the dark. By the time it's light the jars are in rows.
b : Early start. | Same every day? | You've a system.
a : Every day. I'm used to it. | Same. Except Restday. | I have. Rows and order. Without them I'd fall apart.

scene square_dusk
when topic=day|work here.time=dusk self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Packing up. Every jar back in the crate, one by one. | Long day. They're all long. I'll sleep well. | Sold most of the plum. The quince never moves. I don't know why I make it.
b : Tired? | You'll sleep well. | Quince is good, though.
a : Always. It's the honest kind of tired. | I will. And be back at the pan before light. | I think so too. Nobody else does.

scene tired_wolf
when topic=health
does tell.self / sympathise / assess
a : I'm tired. Not ill. Just tired, the way you get. | Folk say I look worn. I am worn. I don't mind it. | Standing all day and boiling half the night. It catches up.
b : You should rest more. | #sympathy# | You need a day off.
a : I should. I won't. But I should. | Don't be. It's the work, and I like the work. | Restday. I sleep through half of it.

scene travellers_trade
when topic=player|newcomer self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : A traveller bought three jars for the road at dawn, and gave me the news from the towns for free. | Strangers buy more than the locals. They don't know what they're missing till they taste. | There's a new face by the square. Looks like a jar of jam would do them good.
b : News and coin at once. | Locals know your jam, though. | Offer one, then.
a : The best kind of customer. | They do. And they come back, which is better. | I might. I'll wait till they look this way.

scene newcomer_good
when topic=newcomer self.role=merchant
does give.opinion / agree / assess
a : There's been a new household come to the street. That's good for the square. | I don't mind new faces. They've all got a sweet tooth, somewhere. | Somebody moved in by the Market Hall. Bought two jars on the first day.
b : More coin about. | That's true. | Two on the first day?
a : More coin, more talk. Both welcome. | Everybody does. You only have to find it. | Plum and quince. The quince. Somebody likes it.

scene wagons_late
when topic=caravan world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : The fruit hasn't come. Every day late is softer plums. | Nothing in off the roads. My pan's sitting cold. | Late carts. I'll have to boil twice as fast when they come.
b : They'll be in soon. | Cold pan, cold stall. | You'll manage.
a : Soon's not soon enough for a plum. | That's it exactly. | I will. I'll not sleep, but I'll manage.

scene wagons_in
when topic=caravan|harvest world.wagons=in self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : Fruit's in. Plums, a few pears. I'll be at the pan till midnight. | The carts came in full. I bought more than I can boil. | Good fruit this week. Firm. Not a bruise on it.
b : Busy, then. | More than you can boil? | That's lucky.
a : Busy. I like busy. | I'll manage it. I always say that. | It is. Firm fruit, good jam.

scene wagons_taken
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided
does tell.worry / sympathise / assess
a : Somebody's robbed a cart. Was it fruit, I wonder. | Robbed again. I hope the carter's all right. The fruit doesn't matter. | Another cart gone. Somebody's going hungry for it.
b : It might not have been fruit. | So do I. | #sympathy#
a : It might not. I'll know by the price next week. | That's the main thing. | Somebody always does.

scene times_hard
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Folk buy one jar where they'd buy two. I don't blame them. | I've a basket of cracked jars at the end. Half price. They go first. | Hard times. When purses tighten, jam goes before bread.
b : They'll come back. | Half price? | It's a treat, jam.
a : They will. Jam keeps. So do I. | Cracked lids. Same jam. I won't throw good jam out. | It does. And still I make it.

scene times_good
when topic=times world.times=good|boom self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : Good weeks. The trestle's bare before noon, most days. | Folk are buying three jars and not looking at the price. | Coin about. I've ordered another pan.
b : Good for you. | Not looking? | Another pan?
a : It's good. I'll make more. I'll be more tired. | Not looking. I look for them. Honest's honest. | Another pan. I'll stand between the two of them like a fool.

scene gossip_glad
when topic=gossip|news topic.claim_kind=marriage|promise_kept|newcomer self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : I heard at the stall: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's nice to hear. | A customer passed it on: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I was glad of it. | Here's a good one. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#.
b : That is nice. | #gladnews# | Good.
a : It is. Good news sells jam. | It is. Past that, it's for others to tell. | Good. Something sweet.

scene gossip_grim
when topic=gossip|crime topic.claim_kind=death|violence|theft|wanted self.role=merchant
does tell.news / sympathise / assess
a : Over the trestle this morning: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I don't like it. | I had it early. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. That's all I know. | There's talk on the square. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#.
b : That's grim. | #pity# | Bad business.
a : Grim. Enough said about it. | Grim. It stays well clear of my jars. | Bad. I hope it's wrong.

scene festival_sell
when topic=festival self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Feast days, I sell out by noon. Then I stand there with an empty trestle. | Festival tomorrow. I've been boiling all week for it. | Everyone's buying for the feast. I'll be ink to the elbow by dark.
b : Go and enjoy it, then. | All week? | That's good trade.
a : I might. I'll be asleep by the music, likely. | All week. The day after, I'll sleep through. | It is. Good trade and sore paws.

scene faith_bees
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The Bright One made bees. That's proof enough for me. | Restday I'm in the chapel, back pew, nodding off. | Every feast a jar goes to the chapel. That's the one prayer I know how to make.
b : Bees? | Nearly? | That's a good prayer.
a : Bees, and honey. You don't get that by accident. | The quiet does it. Best sleep of the week. | It's an honest one.

scene autumn_fruit
when topic=season|harvest here.season=autumn self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Autumn. Plums, damsons, the last pears. My busiest weeks. | The kitchen smells of fruit and smoke and honey. It'll smell like that till winter. | Everything comes at once in autumn. The pan never cools.
b : Busy time. | Sounds lovely. | You'll be tired.
a : Busy. Best time of year. | It is. It's in my fur. | I'll be tired. I'm always tired.

scene winter_shelf
when topic=season|food here.season=winter self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Winter. The shelf's full and the fruit's done. Now I sell what I made. | This is what the jars are for. Plums when the snow's down. | The rows are going down. By spring there'll be gaps.
b : Plums in winter. | Gaps by spring? | Good planning.
a : Taste of autumn in the dark. That's what folk pay for. | Gaps. Then the fruit comes and I start again. | It's not planning. It's just the year.

scene wharf_avoid
when topic=ships|memories self.age>=25 here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The wharf and I are strangers now. Have been for years. | I'll buy salt anywhere but the wharf warehouses. Even if it's dearer. | Ships, cranes. Leave the wharf out of it, if you would.
b : Fair enough. | Even dearer? | I'll leave it.
a : It is. Some places hold things. | Even dearer. It costs me. I'll pay it. | Thank you. Talk to me about plums.

story fell_cook [topic=work|memories self.age>=25 self.role=merchant]
  : I learned the trade from a cook in a Fell kitchen. A big old wolf with burns up both arms.
  : First day, I reached for the sugar. The cook took the sack and poured it in the gutter. Said honey, or go home.
  ask : Sugar down the gutter. Would you have stayed?
  b : Gone home, probably. But you stayed.
  : I stayed. I've not touched sugar since. And I'd pour it out myself now.

story brother_wharf [topic=memories|ships self.age>=25]
  : There was a brother, once, who hauled on the wharf. A crane let its load go and he was beneath it.
  : The House that owned the crane sent a clerk with a purse. Not a word. Just a purse.
  ask : Do you think a purse settles a thing like that?
  b : No. Nothing settles that.
  : No. The chapel had the purse. My feet haven't touched the wharf since.

story burst_jar [topic=work|memories self.age>=20 self.role=merchant]
  : My first summer, a jar burst on the trestle in the heat. Plum all over the cloth.
  : I hadn't boiled the jar long enough. Something was in there, working.
  b : Messy. Did it scare the customers?
  : One laughed. One bought a jar anyway. I boil every jar twice now, whatever the weather.

story mist_empty [topic=weather|memories self.age>=20]
  : One dawn the mist lay so thick on the square, my own paw went grey in front of me.
  : I set out every jar by feel. Turned every label by feel. Not one customer till noon.
  ask : Guess how I spent the morning?
  b : Slept on the trestle, I'd hope.
  : Near enough. I stood in the mist and listened to the city wake up. Best morning I can remember.

story rat_wax [topic=animals|home self.age>=20]
  : A rat once chewed through the wax on a whole row of jars. Eleven of them.
  : I sat up three nights with a broom. On the fourth I caught it, and it glared like I was the thief.
  b : What became of it?
  : Down to the river in a sack and out again. Then home, and every jar up a shelf.

rule @bark_cry
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant here.time=day
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Jams and jellies. Honey-made, every one. | Plum, quince, bramble. Made with honey, not sugar.

rule @bark_turn
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 8h
say There. Face the front. | Who turned that one round?

rule @bark_rain
when concept=bark here.weather=rain self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Jars back from the edge. | Rain. Keep it off the wax.

rule @bark_dusk
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Last jars, and then I'm done. | Cracked lids, half price. Before I pack up.

rule @bark_mist
when concept=bark here.weather=fog here.time=dawn
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Mist. Good. Quiet. | Can't see the Hall. Lovely.

rule @bark_tired
when concept=bark here.time=dusk|night
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Long day. They're all long. | My back. Mm.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. What's wanted? | #greet#. Go on. I'm listening.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Back again. Good. | #greet#, #topic.player#. Same as last time?
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late. I'm tired. Quickly, then. | #greet#. I'm packing up. What is it?
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Say your piece. I've jars to see to. | Go on. But hands off the trestle.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant] : I make jams and jellies and sell them off a trestle on Market Square. Honey, not sugar. | #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Plums, quinces, whatever's in. Boiled at night, sold by day.
dlg @2 resident.job : Order. Everything in its right spot. Quiet work, mostly. | Long days. I don't mind them.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Selling out most days. I can't boil fast enough. | Very well. The jars go as fast as I set them out.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. A jar here, a jar there. It adds up. | Steady. Nothing spoils, nothing's left over. I'm content.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Poorly. Folk aren't buying jam. I don't blame them. | Badly. The jars sit. I keep turning them, and they keep sitting.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : A traveller at the trestle brought it from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That's just as it was told me.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : All the square talks of is #world.event_words#. I've had it all day across the trestle.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Somebody's robbed a cart beyond the walls. If you've no need to go, don't.
dlg @4 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Plums are in. That's my news. | Nothing. A quiet week. I like those.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : Over the trestle, I heard #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've nothing to add.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : What I hear stays at the stall. | Nothing worth repeating.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Mind the cobbles.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Get home safe. | Good night. I'm for bed myself.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop=open] : #greet#. Jams, jellies, a few pickles. All honey. Look all you like. | #greet#. Plum's fresh this week. Quince, if you're brave.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop=open topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Another jar? The plum's put aside for you. | #greet#, #topic.player#. Same as before, or something new?
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #shop_bare# The fruit's late and the shelf's empty. I'll buy, though, if you've honey. | Nearly bare, I'm afraid. A few cracked lids. Half price.
dlg @1 shop.prices : The lid says the price. No haggling. #shop_offer# | Honey's dear, so the jam's dear. It's worth it. #shop_offer#
dlg @1 shop.bye : #shop_bye# Keep it somewhere cool. | #farewell#. Wet spoon, ruined jar. Remember.
graft resident.hub
choice Why do you keep turning the jars? [self.role=merchant] -> labels
node labels
say So the labels face the front. Folk pick them up and put them down any way. I turn them back. All day.
mood calm
free
choice Why does it matter? -> labels_why
choice Never mind. -> hub
node labels_why
say A jar with its back to you looks like it's hiding something. Mine hide nothing. Fruit, month, honey. It's all on the front.
choice Fair enough. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Why honey and not sugar? -> honey
node honey
say Sugar makes it sweet. Honey makes it taste of the fruit. And it keeps. I learned that from a cook who'd pour sugar in the gutter.
mood calm
free
choice In the gutter? -> honey_gutter
choice Let me see your wares. -> greet
node honey_gutter
say Straight in. My first day. I've not touched sugar since. Here, taste the plum, and you'll see why.
choice Let me see your wares. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
